:Namespace QKHULL

⍝ Quickhull is an algorithm for computing the convex hull of a set of points. 
⍝ That is, we are looking to discover the set of points in an n-dimensional 
⍝ space that, when connected by edges, fully encloses all of the points. 
⍝ 
⍝ Quickhull is a classic divide-and-conquer algorithm that is a lot like 
⍝ quicksort. Here's the basic idea (taken from Wikipedia's article that you 
⍝ can find in the references\ directory):
⍝
⍝ 1. Find the points with minimum and maximum x coordinates, as these will 
⍝    always be part of the convex hull. If many points with the same 
⍝    minimum/maximum x exist, use the ones with the minimum/maximum y, respectively.
⍝ 2. Use the line formed by the two points to divide the set into two subsets 
⍝    of points, which will be processed recursively. We next describe how to 
⍝    determine the part of the hull above the line; the part of the hull below 
⍝    the line can be determined similarly.
⍝ 3. Determine the point above the line with the maximum distance from the 
⍝    line. This point forms a triangle with the two points on the line.
⍝ 4. The points lying inside of that triangle cannot be part of the convex hull 
⍝    and can therefore be ignored in the next steps.
⍝ 5. Recursively repeat the previous two steps on the two lines formed by the 
⍝    two new sides of the triangle.
⍝ 6. Continue until no more points are left, the recursion has come to an end 
⍝    and the points selected constitute the convex hull.
⍝
⍝ Since we are dealing with floating point values, we have a choice about 
⍝ comparison tolerance to consider. For our purposes, it's a little easier 
⍝ to just set ⎕CT←0.

⎕CT←0

⍝ To help you play around, we have a set of datasets available in the input\
⍝ directory that you can use to determine whether or not your algorithm is 
⍝ doing the right thing or not.

dataset←⊂'input/1M_rectangle.dat'  ⍝ Roughly 25 points, (+,-)1
dataset,←⊂'input/1M_circle.dat'    ⍝ Roughly 335 points (+,-)1
dataset,←⊂'input/1M_quadratic.dat' ⍝ Roughly 124600 points (+,-)30

⍝ The number of points on the hull is subject to floating-point tolerance and 
⍝ order of operations, so as long as you are getting very close to the number 
⍝ of points you see above, you can consider yourself correct. The suggested 
⍝ tolerance range is given above. 
⍝
⍝ To run the code, we'll have the following runner, which takes an index into 
⍝ dataset above. It reads in the datapoints from the dataset and then calls
⍝ qhull on the points. 

Run←{
	⎕←'Initializing data from ',(⍵⊃dataset),'...'
	tie←(⍵⊃dataset)⎕NTIE 0
	points←(((⍴points)÷2),2)⍴points←⎕NREAD tie 323 ¯1 0
	
	⎕←'Running QuickHull...'
	start←24 60 60 1000⊥¯4↑⎕TS
	res←qhull points
	end←24 60 60 1000⊥¯4↑⎕TS

	⎕←⍴⊃,∘⍪⌿res
	⎕←'QuickHull time elapsed: ',(⍕(end-start)÷1000),'secs'
	1:shy←res
}

⍝ The points data is put into a two-column matrix of x and y values. 
⍝ While this is the format that you get the data in, feel free to change 
⍝ the way that you work with the data if you feel that it would help you 
⍝ write your iteration more clearly or result in faster code. 
⍝ We have included a little timer to help you figure out whether your code 
⍝ is faster or slower than any other design. 
⍝
⍝ Here's a suggested start on qhull:
⍝   L	Set of points ordered by their x value
⍝   R	Boolean vector describing subgroups of L
⍝   K	Keys describing the same information as R

qhull←{L←⍵[⍋⍵[;0];] ⋄ R←1⌽(≢L)↑1 1 ⋄ K←(+⍀R)-1

⍝ A key insight into this algorithm that allows us to turn this into a simpler 
⍝ iterative algorithm instead of a recursive one is that we can flatten the 
⍝ recursions into working on the divided vertices at the same time, since 
⍝ we can treat one group as the "inverse" of another. That is, if we have a 
⍝ line A←→B that divides our vertices, we can treat the group below the 
⍝ A←→B line as above it from the orientation of a B←→A line. 
⍝ So, the first thing we do is arrange for this to occur by figuring out the 
⍝ initial two groups split along the starting line, and splitting them up so 
⍝ that they appear in reverse order and will "work" on the hull in "reverse" 
⍝ orientation. 

	msk←¯1=×-/((1⊖R1)-R1)[K;]×⌽R1[K;]-L⊣R1←R⌿L
	L←(L⌿⍨~msk)⍪⊖msk⌿L ⋄ R←(≢L)↑R⌿⍨~msk
	
⍝ Now here comes the part you need to do...
⍝ On eacn iteration, you need to find the point that is the maximum distance 
⍝ from the two vertices that "bound" it's vertex group (indicated by R and K), 
⍝ and then add that to the set of vertices on the hull (indicated by R). 
⍝ We can compute the distance of the vertices to their respective lines with 
⍝ the following expression:
⍝ 
⍝ D←(-/D[K;]×⌽R1[K;]-L)÷(0.5*⍨+/×⍨D←(1⊖R1)-R1←R⌿L)[K]
⍝ 
⍝ Here is a nice idiom you might want to keep in mind:
⍝
⍝ M[R⌿⍋K[M←⍒D]]	Elements with maximum distance (D) grouped by key K,
⍝ 			where R is a Boolean vector indicating subgroups.
⍝ 
⍝ Write an iterative algorithm that continues to do this until we are done 
⍝ adding vertices to the hull! 

	∘∘∘ ⍝ You implement the iteration here!

⍝ At the end, if R contains all of our vertices on the hull, we can return 
⍝ those like so:

	R⌿L
}

:EndNamespace
